Personally, I think it makes better since to keep it always chaotic.

> Still, since they are Convicts, maybe allowing
> of a Neutral Dwarf Convict might make sense?

I think any race that's normally lawful or neutral might
be chaotic as a criminal, so you could allow for example
a chaotic dwarf convict even if no other role can be a
chaotic dwarf.  However, this should mean monsters
that might be peaceful to a lawful or neutral are hostile.
Notably, lawful dwarves should be hostile to a chaotic
dwarvish convict, IMO.  In addition to making sense,
that also keeps the Mines tricky, as you intended.
(The infravision would help some, but I don't think
that would be overpowered.)

> > You could put the ball in the morning star weapon class.
>
> I think flail might be more fitting,

Flail makes more sense than morning star, as neither
flails nor the iron ball are spikey.

> > > Makes Minetown a lot harder.
>
> > Makes the whole early game a lot harder. The consequence
> > for the early game is that this class can use only the loot that
> > is lying around, which is a really hard restricting factor, in
> > conjunction with the disability to price-ID anything.

The inability to price-check anything at all (which means
among other things that you can't price-ID scrolls of identify
nor figure out which spellbooks you can safely read to get
the identify spell) is probably going to mean that the convict
won't be able to use anything that needs more identification
than pet-checking can provide, until he finds a way to deal
with the shirt.  With the poor starting alignment and luck,
I'm pretty sure that means pet-checking body armor or a
cloak -- and finding a shop that's not in Minetown.

> It is not so easy with the guards who recognize his
> face, so he needs to polymorph or be unseen in some
> manner to walk around Minetown safely.

In practice, this is very likely to push the Minetown trip
rather later into the game.  Invisibility and polymorph
are both rather unlikely to be discovered before you
find some way to ID things, so that's probably in most
cases going to mean you have to find a suitable shop
elsewhere in the dungeon first.  As a hedge against
there not being one anywhere in the main dungeon
(which is unlikely but theoretically could happen),
it might be a good idea to put a general store in the
quest, probably on the middle level ("quest locate",
the wiki calls it).

Incidentally, I think the quest should involve breaking
into a prison (with lots of hostile prison guards, cells
with iron bars, and other colorful style elements) to
free a death-row inmate for some reason or another.


